# Getting Started, part 5: the Tutorial, short sessions each with a document.
.h1 The Tutorial: before you begin [begin]
Each session of this tutorial teaches one part of Strata, and each takes about
a quarter of an hour.  You can read them in any order, but the first two are
the ones everything else uses.  The sessions use three small documents that come
with Strata, {c:memo.doc}, {c:report.doc} and {c:showcase.doc}.  They are in the
directory {c:share/strata/tutorial} of the prefix directory, with the pictures
that they name in the directory {c:pictures} next to them.  Copy the whole
directory to a place of your own, so that you can change the documents; after an
install under {c:/usr} that is:
.code
cp -r /usr/share/strata/tutorial ${{HOME}}/tutorial
.end
.h2 What the Tutorial needs [tutorial-need]
Strata, installed as described in this document.  The sessions about equations,
pictures and Preview need LaTeX; the page that says so says which.  Most of the
screens in this tutorial are the X11 version; the terminal version shows the
same text and boxes, and shows keycaps, pictures and equations as markers.
.h2 How the sessions are written [how]
A line that starts with a number is a step to do.  A key in a box is a key to
press.  The word {b:choose} means to move to a menu entry or a field and press
{k:Ins}.  If you get lost, {k:Esc} backs out of anything and {k:F1} shows help.

.h1 Session 1: Starting with text [s1]
You will make a document, type in it, change how the text looks, and save it.
.h2 Making a document [s1-new]
1. Start the Document Manager.
2. Choose {b:Edit / Preview / Print Document}.  The panel starts on the
   {b:Document Name}.  Type {c:practice.doc} and press {k:Ins}.
3. The file does not exist, so a question follows whether the new document is
   to be structured or unstructured.  Choose {b:Structured}.
4. A panel has fields for a description and an author.  Press {k:Ins}; they may
   stay empty.

The editor opens on an empty document.  The top line is the status line.  The
dotted line below it is the ruler, and the cursor is in the first block.  A
structured document starts with a section whose first block is its headline.
.h2 Typing [s1-typing]
1. Type {c:Notes on the new filing system} and press {k:Enter}.  The headline
   is bold, because that is how this kind of text looks by default.
2. Type a sentence of your own.  When a line fills, the text wraps by itself;
   press {k:Enter} only at the end of a paragraph.

Every time you press {k:Enter} the text ends a line, and the little mark at the
end of the line is the sign of it.  Mistakes are mended with {k:Backspace},
which deletes the character before the cursor, and {k:Del}, which deletes the
character under it.  {k:Ctrl-D} deletes the next word.  {k:Ctrl-/} takes back
the last change that you made, a word that you typed or a paste, and
{k:Alt-/} makes it again; {b:Tools}, {b:Undo} and {b:Tools}, {b:Redo} do the
same.
.h2 Moving about [s1-moving]
The cursor keys move by a character or a line.  {k:Ctrl-Left} and
{k:Ctrl-Right} move by a word, {k:Home} and {k:End} go to the start or the end
of the line, and {k:Ctrl-Home} and {k:Ctrl-End} to the start or end of the
block.  {k:PgUp} and {k:PgDn} move about half a screen.
.h2 Bold, italic and underscore [s1-attributes]
1. Press {k:Ctrl-B}, type a few words, and press {k:Ctrl-B} again.  Those
   words are bold.
2. Do the same with {k:Alt-I} for italics and {k:Ctrl-U} for underscore.
3. {k:Ctrl-N} puts the text back to normal.

A change of attribute takes no room on the screen, but you can delete it
like a letter.
.h2 Menus and panels [s1-menus]
Press {k:F12}.  A bar of menus appears at the top and a line under it lists the
commands of the menu that is lit.
.pic pictures/stred-menu.png 5.5 The menu bar, with File lit.
Move with {k:Left} and {k:Right} over File, Global, Edit, Print, Tools
and Quit, press {k:Enter} to open one, and move through its commands the same
way.
.pic pictures/stred-file-menu.png 5.5 The File menu.
A command opens a panel: a list of fields with a name on the left and a value
on the right.
.pic pictures/stred-save-panel.png 5.5 The panel of File, Save.
{k:Up} and {k:Down} move between fields.  You type over a text field; for a
field with choices, {k:Left}, {k:Right} or the space bar change the choice.
{k:Ins} accepts the panel and {k:Esc} cancels it.  In a field that holds the
name of a file, {k:Keypad +} lists the files of the directory so that you can
choose one.  The bottom of the panel lists the keys.
.h2 Saving [s1-save]
1. Press {k:Ctrl-S}.  A document that has no name yet shows the Save panel; this
   one has a name, so it is saved at once.
2. The message line shows what was done.  The old version of a document is
   kept as a numbered file in the folder {c:.backup} next to it, such as
   {c:.backup/report.bak01}, whenever a changed document is saved ({b:Backups
   Kept} sets how many), so that one mistake is never the end of your work.  The
   Document Manager entry {b:Restore Backup} brings one back.
.h2 Leaving [s1-quit]
Press {k:Ctrl-Q}.  If the document has changes that are not saved (a star shows
in front of its name in the status line), a menu gives the choice to save and
quit, discard the changes and quit, or cancel.  You are back at the Document
Manager.

.h1 Session 2: Sections and the outline [s2]
A structured document is an outline.  You will fold and open the sections of
{c:report.doc}, add sections, and move them about.
.h2 Opening the report [s2-open]
1. In the Document Manager choose {b:Edit / Preview / Print Document} and open
   {c:report.doc}.

Each headline has a number before it, made from its place in the outline.  The
headlines of the sections below a section are indented.
.h2 Folding and unfolding [s2-fold]
1. Press {k:Alt-1}.  Only the headlines of the first level are shown.
.pic pictures/stred-outline.png 5.5 Alt-1: only the first level of headlines.
2. Press {k:Alt-2}.  The second level opens as well.
.pic pictures/stred-outline-open.png 5.5 Alt-2: the second level is open too.
3. {k:Alt-0} shows the headlines of every level; press it again to show the text
   of the whole document, and a third time to go back to the headlines.
   {k:Alt-3} to {k:Alt-9} show the headlines down to that level.  A mark in the
   line above a headline shows whether the section has sections below it ({c:+})
   or not ({c:*}).
4. Put the cursor on a headline and press {k:Keypad -} to fold its section, and
   {k:Keypad +} to open it.  On a terminal these keys are {k:Alt--} and
   {k:Alt-+}.  {k:Keypad *} (or {k:Alt-*}) shows the text of the blocks of the
   section.
.h2 Moving between sections [s2-moving]
{k:Shift-Up} goes to the headline of the section, {k:Shift-Down} to the next
headline, and {k:Shift-PgDn} and {k:Shift-PgUp} to the next and the previous
section of the same level.  {k:Shift-Home} and {k:Shift-End} go to the start and
the end of the document.  {k:Alt-F7} opens the {b:Go To} menu: the start or the
end of the document, a section number that is typed in, and the next or the
previous section.
.h2 Adding and moving sections [s2-adding]
1. Put the cursor in the section {b:Costs}.
2. Press {k:Ins}.  A new section of the same level appears below.  Type a
   headline.
3. Press {k:Shift-Ins} to add a section below it, one level deeper.
4. {k:Alt-]} moves the section to the right, one level deeper, and {k:Alt-[}
   moves it back to the left.  A section can only go deeper when a section of
   its own level stands before it; if not, the message line shows that.

The numbers change as sections come and go.  A cross-reference marker (Session
5) follows the section that is named, wherever the section goes.
.h2 Working on one section [s2-one]
1. Press {k:Ctrl-^}.  Only the section the cursor is in, with the sections
   below it, is shown, and the status line shows {c:SECTION EDIT}.
2. Press {k:Ctrl-^} again to bring back the whole document.

.h1 Session 3: Blocks and formats [s3]
The text of a section is made of blocks.  A block is a paragraph, or a list
item, or a line of data; it is the unit that you copy, move and format.
.h2 Cutting, copying and pasting [s3-cut]
1. Put the cursor in a block of {c:memo.doc}.
2. Press {k:F3}.  A menu lists what to copy: Text, Column, Block or Section.
3. Choose {b:Block}.  The block is highlighted.
4. Move the cursor to the place for the copy and press {k:F5}.

{k:F4} cuts instead of copying, and {k:F7} moves.  The highlight can be made
larger or smaller with the cursor keys; {k:Enter} accepts it and {k:Esc}
cancels it.  {k:Ctrl-K} cuts a block at once.
.h2 Splitting blocks [s3-split]
{k:F2} opens a menu to split a column, a block or a section; {k:Ctrl-V} splits
the block at the cursor.  {k:Alt-F3} joins blocks or sections (on a terminal,
press {k:Ctrl-F} and choose {b:Join}).
.h2 The format of a block [s3-format]
1. Put the cursor in the headline of a section and press {k:F6}.
.pic pictures/stred-format-panel.png 5.5 The first question of Format (F6).
Format puts one question after the other: the left indent of the block, the
justification (left, right, center or even), the indent (none, first, all but
first or all), the attributes (such as normal, bold or italics) and the tab
stops.  {k:Enter} accepts an answer and goes on to the next question, and
{k:Esc} stops.  What you answer is the block's own format; what you do not
change stays as the global format has it.
2. Press {k:Enter} at the left indent, choose {b:Center} in the menu of the

justification, and press {k:Enter} at the questions that follow.
The settings for the whole document, and for each level of sections, are in
{b:Global}, {b:Format}.
.pic pictures/stred-global-format.png 5.5 Global, Format: the document settings.
{k:PgDn} and {k:PgUp} turn through the panels of the levels.  A level takes the
global setting unless you change it.

.h1 Session 4: Columns and tables [s4]
A block can have several columns, side by side.  A block of columns is a row of
a table, and the blocks below it can be more rows.
1. In {c:memo.doc} put the cursor in a block and press {k:F8}.
2. Choose {b:Add column}.  The block has a second column to its right, with a
   colon between the columns.
3. Type in each column.  {k:Shift-Left} and {k:Shift-Right} move from one column
   to the next.
4. Press {k:Ctrl-A} at the end to append another row with the same columns.

{b:Tools}, {b:Autosort}, {b:Table} sorts the rows of a table, by their
first column; {b:Tools}, {b:Count} gives the number of words in the document.
.h2 A table in the showcase [s4-showcase]
{c:showcase.doc} has a table of three columns.  Open it and look at it with
{k:Shift-Right}, {k:Shift-Left} and {k:Ctrl-Home}.  When you print it the
columns become the columns of a table, with a rule at the left of each.

.h1 Session 5: Markers [s5]
A marker is a mark in the text that stands for something else: a footnote, a
key, an equation, a picture, a page break.  Markers are what make a Strata
document more than text.  This session uses {c:showcase.doc}.
.h2 The marker menu [s5-menu]
Press {k:Alt-F12} (on a terminal, {k:Ctrl-F12}).  A panel lists the markers in
three columns.
.pic pictures/stred-marker-menu.png 5.5 The marker commands.
Choose one with {k:Ins}, or press the first letter of its name and {k:Ins}.  If
the marker has fields, a panel holds them; if
you press {k:Alt-F12} on a marker that is already in the text, the panel shows
its fields so that you can change them.
.h2 Keycaps [s5-keycap]
1. Put the cursor in a sentence and press {k:Alt-F12}.  Choose {b:Keycap}.
2. Type {c:Ctrl-S} in the field {b:Legend}.  The other fields show the settings
   of the document in brackets.  Press {k:Ins}. In the window the legend appears
   in a rounded box like a key; in a terminal it is shown between brackets.  The
   keys in this manual are keycaps.  In the panel the foreground, background,
   scale and font are shown in brackets: they come from the keycap format of the
   document, which {b:Global}, {b:Marker} sets for all the keycaps at once.
.h2 Equations [s5-equation]
1. Choose {b:Equation}.  A full screen editor opens, headed {c:EQUATION EDIT}.
2. Type the text of the equation: {c:\frac{{a}}{{b}} + x^2}.  {k:Ctrl-W} shows
   how the equation looks (in a terminal a message states that viewing an
   equation needs the window version); {k:Ins} saves it and {k:Esc} restores
   what it was.
3. In the window, an image of the equation appears in the line.  (It needs LaTeX
   and {c:dvipng}; without them the marker keeps its label.)

The text is TeX.  The sum in the showcase is typed as {c:\frac{{n(n+1)}}{{2}}}.
.h2 Footnotes and notes [s5-note]
Choose {b:Note}.  An editor headed {c:NOTE EDIT} opens in which you type the
text of the note; press {k:Ins} when you are done, or {k:Esc} to give it up.
The note's type is footnote by default; it can also be a levelnote or an
endnote.  The notes are numbered when the pages are made.
.h2 Pictures [s5-picture]
Choose {b:Picture} and type the name of an image file in {b:File Name}: PNG,
JPEG, PDF or EPS.  {k:Keypad +} lists the image files of the directory.  The
panel has a position (fixed, floating or in text), a width, a height, a unit, a
scale, and a name for the table of figures.
.pic pictures/stred-showcase.png 5.5 The showcase: keycaps, equations and a picture.
.h2 Cross-references [s5-xref]
{b:Cross-reference} names a section by its number.  The panel has fields for
the number and a style (the tag, the headline or both); after {k:Ins} the tag
and the headline of the section that was found are shown, and a number that is
not a section is refused with the message that the section number cannot be
found.
.h2 Whitespace and page breaks [s5-space]
{b:Whitespace} sets a vertical space of a size and a unit (the default is
inches), with a treatment that decides what happens to it at the end of a page.
{b:Page Break} starts a new page, an odd page or an even page, and {b:Column
Break} also ends the page.  These show as labels in the text, such as {c:[WS 0.3
in]}, and take effect when the document is printed.
.h2 How markers are shown [s5-modes]
{b:Global}, {b:Marker} has a panel for the markers of the document.  {k:PgUp}
shows the display mode of each marker (most are in short form by default): as a
diamond, as its name, in short form, in long form, hidden, or inline as an
image.  Try the modes on the equation marker to see the difference.

.h1 Session 6: Printing and previewing [s6]
.h2 Print, Draft and Final [s6-draft]
{b:Print}, {b:Draft} and {b:Final} write the document as a PDF file.  Draft is
a quick proof; Final has the line spacing, pictures and title page that the
document asks for.
.pic pictures/stred-draft-panel.png 5.5 The Draft Print panel.
The field {b:Output File} names the PDF, and the field {b:Printer} names a print
queue of your system to send it to (the field shows {c:File only} until a queue
is chosen).  Both need LaTeX.  The other fields hold the number of copies,
whether to print on both sides, which pages or sections, and whether to keep a
log of the errors.  The rest of the {b:Print} menu is for the errors that a
print found, in its submenu {b:Errors}: {b:Next}, {b:Previous}, {b:Remove} and
{b:Remove All}.
.h2 Preview [s6-preview]
Preview shows the document as it will be printed, with its fonts, keycaps,
pictures and equations.  Preview needs LaTeX and an X display.
1. Choose {b:Print}, {b:Preview}, {b:Document}.  The panel has the title page,

the sections to show and the log of the errors.
.pic pictures/stred-preview-panel.png 5.5 The panel that asks what to preview.
{b:Block} previews the block that the cursor is in and {b:Section} the section.
2. Press {k:Ins}.  A panel shows the progress.
.pic pictures/stred-preview-wait.png 5.5 The wait panel of a Preview.
The panel shows the stage, the page that LaTeX is making and the estimated
number of pages, with a bar.  {k:Esc} stops the work.
3. When the pages are ready the panel shows that the Viewer is starting, and a
   window opens with them.
.pic pictures/dviview-page.png 4.5 The Viewer shows the pages of the showcase.
Move with {k:PgDn}, {k:PgUp}, {k:Home} and {k:End}; {k:+} and {k:-} zoom; {k:q}
closes the window and you are back in the editor.
.h2 A PDF [s6-pdf]
Print, Final makes a PDF of the document: the file named in the field Output
File of the panel (chapter 6).
A document that contains an Orientation marker for landscape prints those pages
turned sideways: the Key Reference leaflet is one.
.h2 Fonts [s6-fonts]
A document names its fonts by number.  {b:Global}, {b:Typeface} is the place to
name them in the document, and a font marker changes the font from that point to
the end of the block.  A typeface named {c:Program} is printed in a monospaced
font.  The fonts themselves are in the TeX Font Association of the Document
Manager.

.h1 Session 7: Libraries and macros [s7]
.h2 The library [s7-library]
A library keeps pieces of text that you use again.  Press {k:Ctrl-F3}, choose
what to copy (text, column, block or section), highlight it and press {k:Enter};
a panel has fields for the name of the entry and a description.  {k:Ctrl-F5}
pastes an entry; its name is typed in a panel ({k:Keypad +} lists the names).
{b:Tools}, {b:Library} edits and deletes entries, saves the library in a file
and retrieves one.
.h2 Macros [s7-macros]
A macro is a series of keys that is saved.  Choose {b:Tools}, {b:Macros},
{b:Learn}, type the letter of the macro key (and, if you like, a description)
in the panel, press {k:Ins}, and do the work.  The message line shows that the
macro is being defined.  Press {k:Ctrl-F1} to end the recording.  {k:Alt} and
the key then run the macro.  {b:Save} keeps the macros in a file.

.h1 Where to go from here [next]
The Reference describes everything that a session only touched.  The Key
Reference is a leaflet of the keys; print it, fold it in three, and keep it by
the keyboard.
